lines+=['', 'For the 500 random000 mask, the minimax optimistic stack load rises from 0.570 unrestricted to 0.891 with the existing fore/aft rule; on 500 simulated011 it rises from 0.615 to 0.908. Thus a capacity-feasible mask can materially concentrate local mass and consume slack. Boundary sharing repairs aggregate slot shortfalls, but it does not guarantee local/legal/physical completion.','', 'Five maps require some H cargo on deck even in an optimistic model: heavy footprint demand exceeds all hold cells (000:69>60, 001:68>60, 011:86>60, 100:146>132, 110:135>132). This is a pigeonhole statement about needing deck space, not an infeasibility claim. The legacy hard E6 rule then makes compatible empty hold cells at departure consequential; filling all holds is one sufficient geometric way to avoid that particular failure.','', 'The production outward C2 physics relaxation was attempted on every pinned map. It fails closed before solving with `ValueError: production linear policy epoch is stale; requalify`. No qualification guard was bypassed. Consequently this audit cannot rank trim/list/GM/draught/strength limits from C2 or use it to certify infeasibility. The exact checker/finisher evidence elsewhere in MEDIUM-WALL supplies observed physics failures of particular stows.','', '## Does the generator make a harder problem than a real call?','', 'The evidence supports an artificial tightness mechanism, but no empirical real-call hardness comparison. D36 deliberately scales a full-export booking into the decision region; the source initializer aims around 85% nominal TEU and reserves synthetic ballast against a whole-export mass budget. Here demand is 79–86% nominal TEU and 85–87% of that mass budget. It is not conditioned on a complete loading/checker witness. The producer runs necessary C1 and a background witness, with C2 off; producer acceptance certifies an instance, not a feasible booking. D29 fixes the remaining ship as a synthetic loading condition. Those choices create a dense planning region with fixed surroundings even though simple total capacity checks pass.','', 'The source also retains a legacy hard conditional heavy-under-deck rule while the expert\'s written Q11 describes heavy-under-deck as a preference and permits heavy deck cargo subject to weight order; the pending freeze contract reflects that distinction. This is a concrete domain-model mismatch. Q12 also says special requests may be renegotiated with the shipper when unavailable; these benchmark bookings stay fixed. No benchmark relaxation or source edit is proposed here. There is no cited real call utilization/distribution sample in this audit, so claiming that 80–86% itself is unrealistic would go beyond the evidence.','', 'Local sources: `docs/decisions.md` D29/D36/D77/D102/D184/D185/D191; `src/stowage/generate/random_export.py:ExportPolicy`, `src/stowage/generate/instances.py` booking acceptance; `src/stowage/rules/catalogue.toml` E6; `docs/expert/2026-10-28-written-answers.md` Q11/Q12. These are read-only source observations, not edits or external factual claims.','', '## Constructive follow-up','']
